    "description": "Conjecture: For parametric families of linear and weakly nonlinear elliptic/parabolic PDEs whose discretized solution operators undergo a spectral-gap closing at a control parameter \u03bb = \u03bbc, the smallest width/depth of a neural operator architecture required to achieve fixed relative error \u03b5 exhibits a universal power-law divergence Nmin(\u03b5, \u03bb) ~ C(\u03b5)|\u03bb - \u03bbc|^-\u03bd, where the critical exponent \u03bd depends only on the local universality class of the operator-spectrum singularity (e.g. square-root edge collision, Jordan exceptional point, mobility-edge transition) and not on microscopic discretization details. Test: Construct multiple PDE families with distinct but classifiable spectral singularities, train matched neural operators across resolutions and parameter sweeps, estimate the minimal architecture size needed for target error, and check whether (1) a reproducible power-law divergence appears near \u03bbc, (2) the fitted exponent is stable across discretizations and forcing distributions within a class, and (3) exponents differ systematically between singularity classes; failure of scaling, strong dependence on discretization, or absence of class-specific exponents refutes the conjecture. Impact: This would create a renormalization-style theory of AI solver complexity, predicting when learned PDE surrogates must fail or scale catastrophically near physical phase boundaries, and enabling principled architecture design for scientific machine learning, turbulence closure, materials discovery, and real-time multiphysics control.",
